The federal government denied a request by Massachusetts Gov. Charlie Baker's administration to allow MassHealth not to cover certain drugs.
"While it is disappointing that our request to more effectively control rising pharmacy costs was not approved at this time, we remain committed to finding more innovative state-based solutions to reduce the growth in drug spending while maintaining access to necessary medications," said Massachusetts Health and Human Services spokeswoman Elissa Snook.
Under federal law, state Medicaid programs are entitled to drug rebates, which lower the cost of drugs for the state. In exchange, state Medicaid plans must cover all the drugs approved by the federal government.
Baker's administration had requested flexibility to limit the drugs covered by MassHealth. The state's request said MassHealth would continue to cover at least one drug in a "therapeutic class," and would exclude drugs with "limited or inadequate evidence of clinical efficacy."
In a letter dated Wednesday, the U.S. Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services denied that request. The federal agency wrote that the only way it could grant Massachusetts the authority to exclude drugs would be if Massachusetts withdrew from the Medicaid drug rebate program. Massachusetts gets $900 million annually in drug rebates, of which $550 million is returned to the federal government.
Baker officials say the point of requesting flexibility was to keep MassHealth affordable. Pharmacy spending in MassHealth has doubled from $1.1 billion to $2.2 billion over the last five years. There is a small subset of high-cost drugs that are driving up costs, with more of those drugs in the pipeline.
Snook said efforts to make MassHealth more affordable will continue. "Both CMS and Massachusetts believe in lowering prescription drug costs for public insurance programs and we look forward to continuing our work together toward that common goal," she said.
A CMS spokesman said in a statement, "We... appreciate Massachusetts' efforts to tackle skyrocketing drug prices and look forward to continuing to work with the Commonwealth to meet its goals of effectively managing its Medicaid pharmacy benefit."
The Pharmaceutical Research and Manufacturers of America had opposed Massachusetts' request to limit drug coverage. PhRMA wrote in a letter to federal officials that the proposal "would cut access to lifesaving medicines for patients who have some of the most challenging health circumstances, and cause serious harm to the most vulnerable if it were approved."
There are other proposals pending that could lower MassHealth's drug costs.
The health care advocacy group Health Care for All voiced support for a proposal passed by the state Senate that would set an annual target for pharmaceutical spending and give the state the ability to negotiate steeper discounts.
